==========================================
NETWORKING DAY 12 — WIRESHARK PART 1
Interface, Capture & Display Filters
==========================================

--- STARTING WIRESHARK ---
wireshark &                          # launch the GUI from a terminal
sudo wireshark &                      # if a real interface gives a permission error
sudo usermod -aG wireshark $USER       # permanent fix (log out and back in afterwards)

--- LOCAL TEST SERVER (safe traffic source) ---
python3 -m http.server 8080            # Terminal 1: tiny web server on your own machine
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/ > /dev/null    # Terminal 3: generate a request to capture

--- CAPTURING (GUI) ---
Start screen -> double-click an interface   # Loopback: lo for local traffic, eth0 for your real network
Red square button                            # stop the capture
File -> Save As -> name.pcapng                # save the capture
File -> Open                                   # reopen a saved capture
tcpdump -r name.pcapng -n                       # read the same file from the command line (Day 10)

--- THE THREE PANES ---
Packet List      one line per packet (time, source, destination, protocol, info)
Packet Details   the selected packet broken into layers (Frame, Ethernet, IP, TCP, HTTP...)
Packet Bytes     the raw bytes, hex on one side and ASCII on the other

--- DISPLAY FILTERS (typed in the filter bar, applied AFTER capture) ---
tcp.port == 8080                       # TCP traffic on port 8080 (either direction)
tcp.dstport == 8080                     # only packets going TO port 8080
tcp.srcport == 8080                      # only packets coming FROM port 8080
udp.port == 53                            # DNS over UDP
ip.addr == 192.168.80.136                  # to or from that IP
ip.src == 1.1.1.1  /  ip.dst == 1.1.1.1      # one direction only
http                                          # HTTP packets only
dns                                            # DNS packets only
icmp                                            # ping packets only
arp                                              # ARP packets only
http.request.method == "GET"                      # only GET requests
http.response.code == 200                          # only 200 OK responses
tcp.flags.syn == 1                                  # packets with the SYN flag set (SYN and SYN-ACK)
tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0             # only the first SYN of a handshake
tcp.flags.reset == 1                                  # resets (refused connections)
frame contains "GET"                                   # packets containing that text anywhere

--- COMBINING DISPLAY FILTERS ---
&&   (or "and")      both must match         http && tcp.port == 8080
||   (or "or")       either matches           icmp || dns
!    (or "not")      exclude                   !arp     !(ip.addr == 192.168.80.2)

--- FILTER BAR COLOURS ---
green   valid filter
red     invalid filter (check spelling / syntax)
yellow  valid but possibly not what you meant

--- CAPTURE FILTER vs DISPLAY FILTER ---
Capture filter   set BEFORE capturing, same BPF syntax as tcpdump (port 8080, host 1.1.1.1);
                 packets that don't match are never saved
Display filter   typed AFTER capturing, Wireshark's own syntax (tcp.port == 8080);
                 packets that don't match are only hidden, still in the file

--- QUICK TRICK ---
Right-click any field in the packet details pane -> Apply as Filter -> Selected
   # builds the correct filter for you from a value you clicked on

